// ── Green Key Criteria — full explanatory notes ─────────────────────
// Fuller "Relevance + Expectations for implementation" descriptions per
// criterion, condensed from the official "Green Key Criteria and Explanatory
// Notes, 1 Oct 2026 - 31 Dec 2031". Shown in the application's Description column.
// A brief one-line summary also lives on each criterion's `note` in
// greenKeyCriteria.ts; this map supersedes it for display.

export const GK_FULL_NOTES: Record<string, string> = {
  // ── 1. SUSTAINABLE MANAGEMENT ──────────────────────────────────
  '1.1': 'Appointing a Green Key Establishment Representative guarantees coordination, continuity and communication across all sustainability efforts. Management is actively involved and appoints a Representative from the permanent staff, together with a named substitute to cover absences; both names and contacts are shared with Green Key and updated on any change. The Representative is the primary contact for all sustainability matters (management, staff, suppliers, Green Key and the auditor), instructs and supports colleagues, coordinates training, ensures all performance and business data are collected and submitted, and monitors the strategic targets and annual action plan. Forming a Green Committee with representatives from all departments is strongly recommended; ultimate responsibility lies with senior management. Audit: an interview confirms the Representative can answer questions about the establishment’s sustainability work and Green Key criteria.',
  '1.2': 'Strategic sustainability targets give a goal-oriented framework that goes beyond legal compliance and drives continuous improvement. Establishments with more than 50 employees set at least 4 targets, others at least 2, covering at least 2 different areas (e.g. energy, water, waste, social/CSR, biodiversity, procurement). Targets are grounded in actual performance data from a defined baseline, are formulated up to 6 years ahead, are reviewed each 2-year certification period, and are directly linked to the annual action plan. They are co-developed by management and staff; if a target is not met, a critical reflection on why is provided. Audit: the written, dated targets are presented, shown to be under 2 years old, communicated internally, and developed by staff and management.',
  '1.3': 'The action plan translates the strategic targets into measurable, goal-oriented actions and is the central operational tool of the sustainability management system. It covers the full 24-month certification period with actions divided by year; establishments over 50 employees include at least 4 actions, others at least 2, across at least 2 Green Key areas, each linked to at least one strategic target. Every action is measurable and documents its description, timeline, intended outcome, the responsible department/staff, and the target it supports. Actions are co-developed by management and staff; unfulfilled actions require a documented justification and a plan to complete or replace them. Audit: the written plan is presented and checked for the required number of linked, time-bound actions communicated to staff.',
  '1.4': 'Fair labour practices safeguard employee well-being and reduce inequality. The establishment ensures, for all employees including subcontracted and outsourced on-site staff: pay at or above the legal minimum or an ILO-based living wage; written contracts detailing salary, hours and job description, explained in a language the employee understands, plus payslips; equal pay for equal work regardless of gender, ethnicity or disability, with transparent pay scales; and contracts requiring internally managed subcontractors to meet the same standards. Audit: the auditor samples 3 employment contracts against payroll and pay-structure documentation (and 3 subcontractor agreements where relevant); anonymous interviews are held if irregularities are suspected.',
  '1.5': 'Preventing child labour and protecting minors is a core part of responsible business conduct and human-rights due diligence. No child under 14 is employed under any circumstance, no supplier using child labour is contracted, and national law is followed — or, where weaker or absent, the UN Convention on the Rights of the Child and ILO Conventions 138 (Minimum Age) and 182 (Worst Forms). Where people under 18 work (internships, apprenticeships, paid or unpaid), written guardian/school consent is obtained and all labour laws on hours, rest, leave and insurance are respected. Audit: a signed declaration confirms no under-14 employment (verified visually) plus, if applicable, consent/records for under-18 workers.',
  '1.6': 'Effective grievance and whistleblower systems protect human rights and workplace integrity. The establishment implements a written procedure explaining how to report a concern, who receives it, the follow-up steps, timelines and assurances of confidentiality and non-retaliation; at least 1 confidential or anonymous channel (e.g. an external whistleblower platform); staff information so all workers know how to report; and guest reporting options in guest materials. This criterion applies to establishments with more than 50 employees; smaller ones are encouraged to use proportional options. Audit: the procedure, proof of staff communication, the confidential channel and guest-facing materials are presented, and at least 1 staff interview confirms awareness.',
  '1.7': 'A safe and supportive working environment underpins employee health, satisfaction and performance. The establishment provides written health & safety policies and emergency procedures that are accessible and clearly communicated to all staff, and ensures these measures are reflected in practical day-to-day operations rather than existing only on paper. Audit: the written policies and emergency plans are presented, together with evidence (e.g. signage in staff areas) that they are communicated and implemented in daily operations.',
  '1.8': 'Active cooperation with the surrounding community supports environmental education, local heritage and sustainable destination development. Establishments over 50 employees maintain at least 2 active cooperations with relevant stakeholders, others at least 1, with at least one focused on the local/regional area. To qualify it must be an active two-way relationship benefiting both parties, with at least one documented interaction per certification period (e.g. co-organised events, joint projects, staff volunteering during work hours, in-kind support) — passive donations, one-off purchases or one-sided communication do not count. Audit: evidence of the required number of active cooperations (meeting minutes, activity reports, co-developed materials) is presented; first-time applicants may present agreements and planned actions.',
  '1.9': 'Tourism can unintentionally harm Indigenous Peoples, so respectful engagement and informed decision-making are essential where they are present. The establishment first assesses whether Indigenous Peoples or Indigenous cultural assets exist within a 100 km radius; if none, the criterion is Not Applicable. Where they are present, the establishment ensures that any representation of Indigenous people is developed with their consent and fair compensation, that rituals/ceremonies are preserved authentically, that biodiversity or space redesign integrates traditional ecological knowledge, that access to essential resources and culturally significant sites is not restricted, and that free, prior and informed consent is obtained for land/water use. Audit: the presence assessment is presented, plus supporting evidence (guest materials, consultation records, FPIC agreements) depending on the outcome.',
  '1.10': 'Equitable access to recruitment and development reduces bias and strengthens workplace culture. The establishment selects one focus area — either equitable recruitment or equitable development — regardless of ethnicity, gender identity, disability, age, sexual orientation, religion or socio-economic background, and integrates procedures into its policies. It implements at least 1 documented action (e.g. blind CV screening, inclusive hiring/development targets, mentorship for underrepresented groups, or annual DEI training), supported by a written equal-opportunity policy reviewed every 4 years. This criterion is imperative for establishments with more than 50 employees and guideline for smaller ones. Audit: the policy and evidence of at least 1 implemented action are presented.',
  '1.11': 'Inclusive access removes physical, sensory and cognitive barriers so all guests can participate independently. The establishment focuses on at least 1 category of need — physical mobility, visual impairment, or cognitive/hearing impairment — and puts the required minimum measures in place (e.g. for physical: accessible entrance, public toilet, guest room/bathroom, restaurant seating and a meeting room; for visual: high-contrast signage/tactile markers/lighting plus accessible facilities; for cognitive/hearing: an accessibility plan plus at least 2 tangible actions). Service animals are always permitted, and the level of access is communicated on the website; staff are trained during onboarding. Imperative for hotels, conference centres and attractions; guideline for others. Audit: staff-training and website evidence are shown, and the auditor samples accessible facilities on site.',
  '1.12': 'Trade or display of threatened species and unauthorised artefacts drives biodiversity loss and cultural exploitation. The establishment does not harvest, sell, trade or display any plant or animal species (or their parts/products) listed as Vulnerable, Endangered or Critically Endangered on the IUCN Red List — including souvenirs, décor, traditional medicines or live specimens — even where national law would allow it. Historical and archaeological artefacts are only displayed if lawfully acquired and presented for education or culture. Audit: where such artefacts are displayed, provenance and educational purpose are documented; the auditor samples gift shops, receptions and decorative areas.',
  '1.13': 'Using animals for entertainment can compromise welfare and promote exploitation. The establishment does not offer, promote or facilitate any entertainment, excursion or activity involving the exploitation or suffering of animals — on-site or through recommended/contracted third parties. Use of wild animals for shows, staged photos, direct contact/feeding, welfare-compromising rides/races, or off-site attractions known to harm animals is strictly prohibited; domestic animals may take part in welfare-based, educational or culturally rooted activities under proper conditions. Audit: where animal activities exist, a written statement/SOP confirming no exploitation is presented, and a visual inspection checks any on-site animals.',
  '1.14': 'High animal-welfare standards prevent suffering and demonstrate responsible practice. When animals are kept on the premises, the establishment fully complies with national welfare law and, at minimum, the Five Domains Model as defined in ABTA’s Global Welfare Guidance — ensuring good feeding, comfortable living conditions, health care, freedom to behave naturally and positive emotional well-being, while avoiding hunger/thirst, poor conditions, untreated illness, restricted behaviour and stress. A veterinary assessment is conducted at least once a year. Audit: written confirmation that ABTA guidelines and national law are followed (e.g. an SOP with a Five-Domains checklist) plus annual veterinary records are presented; a visual inspection confirms animals show no negative conditions.',
  '1.15': 'A Code of Business Conduct is a foundational CSR element ensuring ethical, lawful and responsible operations. It is a dated internal document covering policies and corporate culture, anti-corruption and bribery, business ethics, corporate governance, human rights and social safeguards (Universal Declaration of Human Rights and UN Convention on the Rights of the Child), and animal welfare where applicable. It is formally approved by management, reviewed at least every 4 years, and shared with all staff (onboarding, handbook, training) and made available on request. A chain-wide code is acceptable if it demonstrably applies to the property and staff are aware of it. Audit: the dated, approved code covering all dimensions is presented, with evidence of staff awareness.',
  '1.16': 'Supporting physical and mental well-being reduces stress and burnout and builds a resilient workplace. The establishment implements at least 1 tangible initiative per year, or maintains ongoing free/subsidised facilities — for example team-building or wellness days, access to exercise facilities, mental-health or stress-management workshops, preventive health initiatives, wellbeing facilities (quiet/prayer/breastfeeding rooms), healthy-habit challenges, or family-friendly measures. Audit: documentation of the initiative(s) (HR communications, programme descriptions, photos, participation records) or evidence of existing facilities is presented, and staff may be interviewed on awareness.',
  '1.17': 'Offering opportunities to people facing employment barriers supports social equity and inclusion. Within the last certification period (or 12 months for first-time applicants) the establishment implements at least 1 of: a traineeship/apprenticeship of at least 3 months for the long-term unemployed; active recruitment, retention and development of staff with disabilities; part-time/flexible contracts for people with limited employability; employment or training for people with limited language skills or from vulnerable backgrounds; or active collaboration with school-to-work or social-support programmes. Roles, hours and conditions are adapted to individuals’ needs. Audit: evidence matching the chosen initiative (contracts, accommodation records, cooperation agreements) is presented.',
  '1.18': 'Building on criterion 1.11, this guideline extends inclusive access to at least 2 categories of need — physical mobility, visual impairment and/or cognitive/hearing impairment — implementing the required minimum measures for each. Service animals are always permitted, the level of access is communicated accurately on the website, and staff are trained on accessibility provisions and guests’ rights during onboarding. Audit: evidence of staff awareness/training and website information is presented, an accessibility plan is shown where cognitive/hearing needs are addressed, and the auditor samples accessible infrastructure across public areas, a restaurant, a meeting room and guest rooms.',
  '1.19': 'Supporting local micro and small entrepreneurs strengthens the local economy, preserves cultural heritage and reduces long supply chains. The establishment provides a platform — a small shop or stand on the premises, or by purchasing and offering the products to guests — for local micro (fewer than 10 staff) or small (fewer than 50 staff) enterprises to sell sustainable products based on the area’s nature, history and culture. Qualifying products are produced within 100 km, respect environmental/social/cultural principles, use no threatened species, and avoid single-use or non-durable materials. Audit: agreements with the local entrepreneurs are presented, and a visual inspection confirms the products are sold or offered.',
  '1.20': 'Regular communication enables staff to understand their role in sustainability and respond to guest enquiries. Management, the Green Key Representative and/or Green Committee brief staff at least 2 times per year (in person, in writing or via e-learning) on ongoing and new initiatives — typically water, energy, waste, cleaning, F&B, circular-economy principles, biodiversity, community engagement and the Green Key programme. This includes internal and outsourced on-site staff; seasonal or ≤5-staff establishments brief at least once a year, and first-time applicants hold at least 1 briefing before the audit. Audit: written evidence of topics, participating departments and dates is presented, and at least 1 staff interview validates awareness.',
  '1.21': 'Role-specific training ensures staff contribute effectively to the sustainability strategy and daily operations. Hotels/hostels and campsites provide 1 general sustainability training per year plus at least 1 departmental/role-specific training; small accommodations, restaurants, attractions, conference centres and seasonal establishments provide at least 1 training per year. Training is progressive (deepening each year rather than repeating), delivered on-site or online, internally or externally, with new staff normally trained within their first 4 weeks. Audit: a document detailing topics, departments, dates/duration and format is presented; first-time applicants present a draft plan; an interview verifies a staff member’s participation and understanding.',
  '1.22': 'Reducing the frequency of towel and sheet changes cuts water, energy and detergent use. In addition to offering guests the option to reuse towels and sheets (criterion 6.21), housekeeping staff are given clear, accessible instructions and guidance on the reuse procedures and correctly apply them; where housekeeping is outsourced, training on the procedures is written into the contract. Audit: during the visual inspection at least 1 housekeeping staff member is interviewed to confirm they know the towel- and sheet-change procedures.',
  '1.23': 'Educational material in staff areas strengthens staff awareness and supports the daily implementation of sustainability practices. The establishment displays informative, visually engaging material — notice boards, posters, stickers, leaflets, newsletters, digital screens or apps — encouraging staff to use resources wisely (switching off lights/taps, using less paper) and to run equipment efficiently (dishwashers, laundry, vacuum cleaners), and, where relevant, addressing local biodiversity and social responsibility such as how to report discrimination. The material is kept up to date. Audit: the auditor confirms suitable material is displayed in staff areas.',
  '1.24': 'Letting staff evaluate environmental and social performance encourages participation and continuous improvement. The establishment runs an annual, structured feedback process (surveys, feedback meetings, or suggestion boxes managed by the Green Key Representative), collects and analyses the results, monitors trends in staff perceptions, shares results with staff, and formulates and communicates responses within 2 months. Feedback may be anonymous and multiple formats are offered for inclusiveness. Audit: evidence of the system, its regular use, an analysis of the latest round and a summary of actions taken is presented, and a staff member is interviewed about the process.',

  // ── 2. GUEST AWARENESS & INVOLVEMENT ───────────────────────────
  '2.1': 'Displaying the certificate builds guest trust and reinforces Green Key as a symbol of sustainability. The establishment displays the current-period Green Key certificate and standard programme information in a highly frequented, guest-accessible area such as reception or the main entrance, using the officially approved logo and the key points from the Green Key Toolbox (design may follow the establishment’s branding). Any additional claims must be objective and reflect only what is currently implemented; if the certificate is suspended, withdrawn or not renewed it is removed immediately. Audit: the display is checked for being current, prominent, accessible and in good condition, and matching the official content.',
  '2.2': 'Website information enhances transparency and reinforces the certificate’s credibility. The establishment’s website contains brief information about its Green Key certification, displays the officially approved logo, and links to the international (greenkey.global) and/or national Green Key website, placed where guests will see it (e.g. booking page or homepage). All communication accurately reflects current practices, does not exaggerate, and uses the official site as the primary source. Audit: the website information is presented and checked for accuracy, relevance, placement and, where appropriate, being multilingual; first-time applicants publish within 6 months of certification.',
  '2.3': 'Clear communication helps guests understand, support and take part in the establishment’s sustainability work. Accurate, easy-to-understand information is displayed consistently across guest touchpoints (public areas, guest/meeting rooms, screens, QR codes, brochures, apps) and, beyond describing policies and achievements, actively invites guests to take specific actions — water/energy conservation, waste sorting, reducing food waste, supporting biodiversity or charities — with a clear, motivational call to action. Multilingual, visually engaging materials are recommended; all claims stay accurate and current. Audit: the information provided to guests, including materials encouraging participation, is presented.',
  '2.4': 'Guest-facing staff play a key role in communicating sustainability values and maintaining the integrity of the certification. All guest-facing staff can clearly explain what Green Key is, describe the site’s main initiatives (e.g. towel reuse, local sourcing, energy saving) and tell guests how to contribute during their stay; a brief verbal or written "sustainability welcome message" at check-in is strongly recommended. Staff do not overstate or misrepresent performance. Audit: during the visual inspection at least 1 guest-facing staff member is interviewed and must provide accurate, clear and understandable information about Green Key and the establishment’s key sustainability actions.',
  '2.5': 'Encouraging sustainable transport reduces carbon emissions and air pollution. The establishment informs and encourages guests, where feasible and safe, to use alternatives to private cars/taxis — local public or private transport, shared taxis/minibuses, taxis with an environmental policy, establishment shuttles, or e-scooters/bikes. The information is accurate, clear and visually engaging, available on the website and/or welcome email (with the address and how to reach the establishment), and may also appear at reception, in-room or on screens/QR codes; its validity is checked at least twice a year. Audit: the communication materials are presented and checked for accuracy, clarity and being up to date.',
  '2.6': 'Guest feedback is essential to understanding and improving the quality and sustainability of operations. The establishment runs a structured process for guests to evaluate its sustainability performance and offer suggestions — inviting participation during the stay, at check-in/out, in person or digitally (email, QR code, app) — then collects, analyses and uses the feedback, identifies trends, and addresses issues promptly where feasible. Audit: evidence of the feedback system, an analysis of the most recent round and documentation of actions taken in response (which may link to the action plan) are presented; first-time applicants present a written plan.',
  '2.7': 'Public sharing of sustainability efforts raises guest awareness, strengthens credibility and encourages wider adoption. The establishment actively communicates its practices, progress and Green Key engagement through its own digital channels (social media, website, newsletters) at key moments — the release of the certificate and at least 1 intermediate update — and is encouraged to follow and amplify official Green Key channels. During each certification year it submits at least 1 verified Good Practice to Green Key for potential promotion and peer learning. Audit: a summary of communication activity, including links or screenshots of posts, is presented.',
  '2.8': 'Information about nearby nature fosters environmental awareness and responsible, nature-based recreation. The establishment offers written information about nearby parks, landscapes, conservation zones, beaches and other ecologically sensitive areas, encouraging outdoor activities such as walking, cycling, swimming, birdwatching or picnicking while following good practice to minimise impacts on biodiversity. It is clearly visible, relevant, visually engaging and placed in guest-accessible areas, ideally multilingual. Imperative for hotels, campsites, small accommodations and attractions; guideline for conference centres and restaurants. Audit: the information provided to guests, including interpretive content and codes of conduct, is presented.',
  '2.9': 'Promoting responsible visitor behaviour helps prevent overtourism, minimises disturbance to communities and fosters respect for local culture. The establishment provides written information promoting responsible behaviour in the destination — referencing national/local tourist-conduct guidelines where appropriate — covering, for example, preventing overtourism, respectful behaviour towards communities and the environment, sightseeing norms, conduct at religious/historical sites, and avoiding littering. It is clear, accurate, up to date and accessible (reception, rooms, digital platforms, booking emails). Imperative for hotels, campsites, small accommodations and attractions; guideline for conference centres and restaurants. Audit: the materials are presented and verified against the requirements and local context.',
  '2.10': 'Awareness-raising activities increase understanding of sustainable development and strengthen engagement with local environmental and social issues. The establishment implements and actively promotes at least 4 awareness initiatives per certification period (roughly 2 per year), including at least 1 environmental (e.g. Earth Day, clean-ups, biodiversity conservation) and 1 social (e.g. charity, commemorations, fundraising), all involving documented guest participation — staff-only activities do not qualify. Recurring awareness days are acceptable if guest participation is documented. Audit: documentation of the activities held in the last full year and those planned for the next two years is presented (dated lists, communication materials, timestamped photos).',
  '2.11': 'Offering non-motorised transport reduces emissions, supports sustainable mobility and promotes guest health. The establishment either provides bicycles or other non-motorised transport (canoes, kick sleds, snowshoes, cross-country skis) to borrow or rent on site, or refers guests to a reliable external rental service; electric bikes and e-scooters do not fulfil this criterion. Information about the available transport is accessible to guests (reception, concierge, environmental corner, screens, apps, QR codes or room binders). Audit: the auditor confirms either the availability and promotion of non-motorised transport, or clearly communicated information referring guests to external rental services.',

  // ── 3. WATER ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  '3.1': 'Monthly recording of water use enables early detection of leaks and inefficiencies and supports data-driven decisions. The establishment records its total water consumption at least once a month, systematically and in a traceable format, documenting the data-collection methodology (utility bills, sub-metres, manual or automated readings) and the source(s) of water. Where water is not from a public supplier or where scarcity restrictions apply, it holds documentation that abstraction is legally authorised. Where monthly data are unavailable, the most precise estimate is provided. Audit: monthly records for the last 2 calendar years per source (3 months minimum for first-timers) and supporting methodology documentation are presented; the auditor samples 3 items.',
  '3.2': 'Dripping and leaking outlets waste water and worsen the environmental footprint. The establishment actively monitors water outlets and repairs leaks without delay under a written SOP that sets check frequency by outlet type: high-usage/high-risk outlets checked each time the area is cleaned, medium-usage weekly, and low-usage at least monthly or before use. Detected leaks and corrective actions are recorded in an incident/maintenance log, and responsible staff know the procedures. Automatic leak-detection is recommended. Audit: the written SOP is presented (plus the incident log where leaks occurred), and the auditor samples a public area, a staff/back-office area and guest rooms.',
  '3.3': 'Reducing shower flow lowers water use in high-consumption guest facilities. Water flow in at least 80% of showers — in guest rooms, public areas, staff areas, spas and gyms — does not exceed 9 litres per minute, achieved through low-flow heads, flow restrictors or aerators (or, for rain showers, certified low-flow heads or pressure regulation). The establishment keeps an overview of all showers and conducts self-checks (≥80% for first-timers, ≥50% of the previous group for re-applicants), documented and updated within 6 months of the audit. Audit: the self-check overview is presented, and the auditor measures shower flow in a public-area shower, a staff shower and guest rooms.',
  '3.4': 'Efficient handwashing taps deliver daily water savings across guest and staff spaces. Water flow in at least 80% of handwashing taps (including sensor taps) in guest rooms, staff areas and public areas such as toilets, spas and gyms does not exceed 8 litres per minute, achieved through built-in restriction, restrictor devices or aerators; kitchen and utility taps are excluded. The establishment keeps a documented overview and conducts self-checks (≥80% for first-timers, ≥50% of the previous group for re-applicants), updated within 6 months of the audit. Audit: the self-check overview is presented, and the auditor measures tap flow in a public area, a staff/back-office area and guest rooms.',
  '3.5': 'Efficient urinals cut water wasted by inefficient flushing. Where urinals are present in public and staff areas, they have individual detection sensors, a push-button flushing no more than 3 litres per flush, or are water-free; water-free systems are maintained per manufacturer specifications. Establishments are encouraged to plan maintenance and staff training as part of procurement. Audit: during the visual inspection the auditor confirms urinals are fitted with individual sensors, restricted push-button flushing, or are water-free.',
  '3.6': 'Water-efficient toilets conserve freshwater and support responsible facility management. Toilets purchased within the past 24 months (6 months for first-time applicants) for guest rooms, public and staff areas are either dual-flush with a maximum of 3/6 litres per flush, or sensor-based allowing a maximum of 4.5 litres, achieved via permanent technical restriction rather than a user-adjustable setting; equivalent or better high-efficiency models are also accepted. Retrofit flush-reducing devices and behavioural nudges are recommended for older models. Audit: invoices for toilets purchased in the relevant period are presented, showing the purchase date and flush volume of each unit.',
  '3.7': 'Dishwashers and laundry machines are major water consumers, so efficient new models reduce impact and cost. Machines purchased within the past 24 months (6 months for first-timers) meet low-water requirements: professional dishwashers ≤3.5 litres per basket (or equivalent) and domestic ones in the top two efficiency classes; professional laundry machines ≤7 litres per kg of linen (or the most stringent locally available) and domestic ones in the top two efficiency classes. Appliance type and capacity match the operational scale and are used and maintained per instructions. Audit: a list of appliances purchased in the relevant period and their water-consumption specifications (technical data, efficiency ratings or supplier data) are presented.',
  '3.8': 'Sub-metering provides the detailed data needed to locate inefficiencies and set meaningful reduction goals. The establishment installs at least 2 additional separate water metres (or equivalent devices, e.g. smart showerheads that record consumption) in high-consumption areas or where sub-metering supports reduction goals; large sites may meter individual parts or rooms. Consumption at each metre is recorded, ideally more frequently than monthly, and integrated into the overall water-tracking system to support better targets. Audit: during the visual inspection the auditor confirms the presence of sub-metres and the monitoring methodology.',
  '3.9': 'Understanding local water risks ensures long-term access, community resilience and environmental protection. The establishment carries out a water risk assessment appropriate to its context and scale, identifying risks such as scarcity/stress, drought, flooding, infrastructure vulnerability and quality issues, analysing current and projected conditions and resulting in a report of risks, exposure and recommended mitigation. Where risks are found, a response plan is developed with relevant stakeholders. The assessment may use recognised tools (WWF Water Risk Filter, WRI Aqueduct, ISO 14046) or public-utility data, and is reviewed at least every 6 years. Audit: the latest assessment (and any response plan) is presented.',
  '3.10': 'Collecting rainwater and/or AC condensate reduces demand for potable freshwater and supports resilience, especially in water-scarce or high-cooling contexts. The establishment collects rainwater and/or AC condensate using tanks, cisterns or open systems and uses it for suitable purposes such as garden irrigation, toilet flushing, cleaning of vehicles/surfaces, cooling systems, laundry, or human consumption after proper treatment where legally permitted. Collection and use are safe, maintained and cause no adverse effects; a metering to track collected volumes is recommended. Audit: the auditor confirms the collection installations, and documentation is presented where the water is used for human consumption.',
  '3.11': 'Going beyond criterion 3.4, this guideline reduces tap flow further. Water flow in all handwashing taps (including sensor taps) in guest rooms, public and staff areas does not exceed 5 litres per minute, achieved through built-in restriction, restrictor devices or aerators; kitchen and utility taps are excluded. The establishment keeps a documented overview and conducts self-checks (≥80% for first-timers, ≥50% of the previous group for re-applicants), updated within 6 months of the audit. Audit: the self-check overview is presented, and the auditor measures tap flow in a public area, a staff area and guest rooms.',
  '3.12': 'Automatic cut-off taps significantly reduce water waste in frequently used public restrooms. All public-area handwashing taps are fitted with automatic water cut-off systems — sensor-activated or push-button — delivering only the water needed per handwashing cycle, configured with appropriate preset flow times (e.g. 5–10 seconds) and kept maintained (battery/sensor calibration). Private guest rooms, kitchen sinks and bidet showers are excluded. Audit: the auditor confirms the presence and functionality of automatic cut-off systems on the eligible public taps.',
  '3.13': 'Bathtubs, jacuzzis, spa tubs and private pools consume far more water per use than efficient showers. The establishment does not provide operational bathtubs, jacuzzi/spa or private pools in any guest rooms or suites; it is acceptable to render existing equipment unusable (e.g. removing stoppers or cutting the water). Replacing in-room tubs and pools with low-flow showers during renovation is one of the most effective ways to cut per-guest water use. Audit: the auditor confirms there are no operational bathtubs, jacuzzi/spa or private pools in guest rooms/suites, or that such rooms cannot be booked.',
  '3.14': 'Evaporation is a significant and often overlooked source of water loss in exposed outdoor systems. For each major outdoor water-using system present — swimming pools, hot tubs, spa tubs, rainwater storage, ponds, fountains, waterfalls — the establishment applies at least 1 evaporation-reduction measure, such as a well-insulated cover used outside operating hours, a liquid/gel barrier, windbreaks/shading, or sealed storage, adapted to the local climate and exposure. Audit: the auditor confirms at least 1 evaporation-control measure is present for each existing system type.',
  '3.15': 'Proper wastewater treatment protects water bodies and communities and meets legal and ethical standards. The establishment is connected to a public sewage system or treats wastewater in its own system (septic, biodigester, constructed wetland or other biological treatment), covering wastewater from rooms, kitchens, bathrooms, laundries, pools, spas and other areas. Treatment complies with national/local law or, absent legislation, the most advanced feasible treatment removing key pollutants; treated water is reused where possible (criterion 3.17). Audit: a licence, connection proof or, for on-site systems, an operating permit/licence and maintenance records — or recent water-quality test results — are presented.',
  '3.16': 'Well-functioning grease traps save water and chemicals and prevent fat, oil and grease from blocking drains. Establishments with a professional or commercial kitchen have at least 1 grease trap serving all relevant drainage points where FOG is discharged (sinks, dishwashers, floor drains); traps are emptied and maintained per manufacturer and local requirements and cleaned at regular intervals to prevent odour or overflow. Collected FOG is handled as hazardous waste (criterion 5.4). Audit: cleaning records or invoices for the last 2 calendar years (1 year for first-timers) are presented, and where possible a visual inspection confirms well-functioning, adequately sized traps.',
  '3.17': 'Reusing treated wastewater reduces freshwater demand and supports climate resilience, particularly in water-scarce regions. The establishment treats and reuses wastewater on-site (or uses treated wastewater from an authorised utility, or supplies its treated wastewater to a third party), using it only for safe non-potable purposes such as irrigation of green areas, toilet flushing or cooling — never for human consumption, direct contact, or irrigation of food crops. Systems are designed and maintained to prevent contamination of potable networks; blackwater reuse requires the relevant permit. Audit: technical documentation or a written protocol describing sourcing, treatment and reuse is presented (a permit is mandatory for blackwater reuse).',

  // ── 4. ENERGY & CARBON ─────────────────────────────────────────
  '4.1': 'Tracking energy by source supports management, cost control and early detection of inefficiencies. The establishment records energy consumption at least once a month by source (electricity, gas, district heating, fuel, renewables) in a traceable format, with the data-collection methodology documented; photos or screenshots are not an acceptable format. Where monthly data are unavailable, the most precise estimate is provided, and separate energy metres are recommended. Major changes or spikes are investigated and corrected. Audit: monthly records for the last 2 calendar years per source (3 months minimum for first-timers) and supporting methodology documentation are presented; the auditor samples 3 items from 3 different months.',
  '4.2': 'Appropriate temperature setpoints optimise comfort while reducing energy use and emissions. The establishment sets a standard temperature for cooling and heating — in guest and meeting rooms for accommodation, and the whole area for restaurants, attractions and conference centres. As a general standard, if the outside temperature is above 22 °C the cooling setpoint is no lower than 22 °C, and if below 22 °C the heating setpoint is no higher than 22 °C, within accepted comfort ranges; guest adjustments are encouraged to stay within about 3 °C of the setpoint. Programmable thermostats and building energy management systems are encouraged. Audit: the written SOP for setting and managing temperature is presented, and the auditor samples setpoints in meeting rooms, guest rooms or public areas.',
  '4.3': 'Switching off unused lighting and equipment avoids wasted heating, cooling and power. The establishment has an automatic or manual procedure to turn off unnecessary appliances and lighting when guest rooms, rental properties and meeting rooms are unoccupied. A manual approach uses a written SOP covering short-term vacancy (e.g. up to 2 nights: lights, AC, TVs off including standby) and low-occupancy periods (e.g. below 75%: consolidating guests, closing wings/floors). Automated solutions include occupancy sensors, motion detectors, guest-room or building energy management systems, or mobile/IoT controls. Audit: where manual, the SOP is presented and a visual inspection confirms the practices; where automatic, the auditor samples a rental property, a meeting room and guest rooms.',
  '4.4': 'Shifting heating, cooling, hot water and cooking to renewables cuts emissions and improves energy independence. At least 50% of the energy used for heating and cooling (and hot water and cooking) is supplied from renewable sources for a minimum continuous period of 3 months — produced on-site or sourced externally, ideally nearby. Acceptable sources include solar thermal, geothermal/heat pumps, biogas and sustainable biomass, wind, hydro, certified renewable electricity powering climate control, and renewable district heating/cooling; peat does not count. Audit: system specifications, invoices/certificates, energy-source disclosures, and calculations demonstrating the 50% share are presented; where energy is produced on-site the systems are visually confirmed.',
  '4.5': 'Building on criterion 4.4, this guideline covers full decarbonisation of thermal energy. All energy used for heating and cooling (and hot water and cooking) is supplied from renewable sources — produced on-site or sourced externally, ideally nearby — using solar thermal, geothermal/heat pumps, biogas and sustainable biomass, wind, hydro, certified renewable electricity, or renewable district systems; peat does not count. Audit: system specifications, invoices/certificates, energy-source disclosures, and calculations demonstrating the full renewable share are presented; on-site systems are visually confirmed.',
  '4.6': 'Renewable and eco-labelled electricity significantly reduces the emissions associated with energy use. At least 50% of the establishment’s total electricity consumption comes from renewable sources — solar PV, wind, hydro, geothermal, biogas or certified sustainable biomass — produced on-site or sourced externally, ideally nearby. Purchasing third-party-verified or eco-labelled electricity with recognised sustainability requirements is encouraged. Audit: contracts, certificates or written proof of the provider(s) and the renewable share, the latest electricity invoice, and guarantees of origin (or equivalent tracking such as RECs/I-RECs) are presented.',
  '4.7': 'Building on criterion 4.6, this guideline covers fully renewable electricity. The establishment uses electricity from renewable sources — solar PV, wind, hydro, geothermal, biogas or certified sustainable biomass — for 100% of its total electricity consumption, produced on-site or sourced externally, ideally nearby, with eco-labelled/third-party-verified electricity encouraged. Audit: contracts, certificates or written proof of the provider(s) and the renewable share, the latest electricity invoice, and guarantees of origin (or equivalent) are presented.',
  '4.8': 'Windows are a significant source of heat loss and gain, increasing heating and cooling needs. At least 75% of the windows on site are energy-efficient beyond national/local regulation — high thermal insulation such as triple glazing in cold climates, or sun-reflecting film, blinds or shading in hot climates — and, in very hot or cold climates, opening may be restricted; thermal curtains or insulating films are recommended where full replacement is not feasible. Audit: a summary describing the thermal features of all windows plus a statement (preferably from an external expert) confirming at least 75% exceed standards is presented, and the auditor samples window types across public areas, corridors, staff areas and guest rooms.',
  '4.9': 'Regular external energy audits reveal where and how energy is consumed, guiding effective savings. The establishment has an external energy audit conducted at least once every 6 years by a qualified independent professional (an energy certificate or declaration alone does not suffice), aimed at reducing total energy use and increasing renewables. The report identifies high-consumption areas and includes an action plan with suggested measures, estimated effects, costs and payback; a thermographic survey is highly recommended. Audit: the report from an audit carried out within the last 6 years is presented.',
  '4.10': 'Improved insulation reduces heat loss and gain, cutting energy use, costs and emissions. The establishment implements at least 1 insulation measure — insulating hot-water pipes or supply/exhaust ducts, thermal insulation of attics, walls, floors or ceilings, or heat-reflective panels behind radiators — chosen based on a professional assessment or documented expert guidance so the most impactful measure is taken. Only sustainable insulation materials are used (e.g. wood wool, cork, hemp, cellulose or other low-emission/recycled materials with verified profiles). Audit: evidence of the insulation work and materials (invoices, installation reports, product specifications or photographs) plus evidence of the professional assessment is presented.',
  '4.11': 'Additional metering provides accurate information to target energy-reduction strategies. The establishment installs additional or separate metres for electricity, gas or other locally relevant sources (e.g. district heating), particularly in high-consumption areas such as kitchens, heated pools, gyms/spas or externally managed businesses. Consumption from each metre is collected and recorded, ideally more frequently than monthly for more detailed information. Audit: during the visual inspection the auditor confirms the presence of sub-metres and the monitoring methodology.',
  '4.12': 'Heating, cooling and ventilation are among the largest energy consumers, so effective control cuts use and emissions. The establishment has a system — centralised or decentralised, fully or partly automated (a building management system), or a written manual procedure — to control ventilation, comfort heating and comfort cooling, using measures such as occupancy- or CO₂-controlled ventilation, reduced operating hours and temperature control. Where possible the premises are divided into independently adjustable zones, and control accounts for seasonal changes and use patterns in line with criterion 4.2. Audit: where manual, the written procedures shared with staff are presented; the auditor confirms an automated or manual building management system.',
  '4.13': 'Well-maintained systems reduce energy use, ensure air quality and comfort and catch refrigerant leaks early. The establishment checks and maintains ventilation, heating and cooling systems at least once a year (preferably by a certified professional, or internally if qualified and legal). Ventilation checks include filter replacement, cleaning of ducts/diffusers/vents, inspection of heat exchangers and airflow verification; heating/cooling checks include inspection of key components and cleaning/replacing filters and heat exchangers. Manual checks are done even with automated monitoring, and any issue is resolved within 2 months. Audit: a maintenance protocol/SOP and reports or checklists from the last 12 months are presented.',
  '4.14': 'Efficient lighting cuts electricity, emissions and operating costs. At least 80% of all light bulbs across guest rooms, public areas (lobbies, restaurants, conference spaces, corridors) and staff areas are LED or a technology matching/exceeding LED in energy efficiency, luminous efficacy and lifespan; incandescent, halogen and CFL do not meet this benchmark and are phased out. Selecting the most efficient bulbs when purchasing is recommended. Audit: documentation (a list or invoices) estimating installed bulbs and their type is presented, and the auditor samples bulb types across a lobby, restaurant, conference space, corridor, staff area and guest rooms.',
  '4.15': 'Managing outdoor lighting reduces energy use, cost and light pollution affecting local biodiversity. The establishment has a system to reduce outdoor-lighting energy — turning lights off during parts of the night and/or installing motion sensors so lighting activates only on detection — adapted to each area’s function and safety needs. Biodiversity-conscious design is applied especially in sensitive areas: shielded downward-directed luminaires, low-intensity warm-colour lighting (<3000K), minimising lighting near habitats, and sensor-based control. Audit: during the visual inspection the auditor checks the adopted lighting solutions, control types and placement, confirming outdoor-lighting reduction measures are in place.',
  '4.16': 'Efficient electronic devices reduce the footprint and operating cost of widely used equipment. Electronic devices purchased within the past 24 months (6 months for first-timers) — such as televisions, kettles, air-conditioning units and fans — are rated energy-efficient on a recognised national or regional label (EU Energy Label, Energy Star, etc.); where no label exists, one of the most efficient options is chosen based on comparing at least 3 comparable models. Imperative for hotels, campsites and small accommodations; guideline for conference centres, attractions and restaurants. Audit: a list or invoices with purchase dates, models and locations plus energy-label/specification data are presented.',
  '4.17': 'Mini bars run continuously, so limiting or improving them cuts electricity demand. The establishment preferably avoids in-room mini bars/compact fridges; where installed within the past 24 months (6 months for first-timers) they have a maximum daily consumption of 0.6 kWh/day or are rated energy-efficient on a recognised label, with natural refrigerants (e.g. R600a, R290) or a GWP below 100 strongly recommended. Where no label exists, technical documentation and a 3-model market comparison demonstrate one of the most efficient options. Audit: a list of units purchased in the relevant period plus specification/energy-label data are presented; the auditor may sample in-room models if irregularities are suspected.',
  '4.18': 'Refrigerators, cold stores, ovens and heating cupboards are significant continuous loads, so regular maintenance keeps them efficient and food-safe. The establishment maintains all such equipment at least once a year (preferably more often), ensuring intact, properly closing door seals with no damage, no excess ice in freezing devices, and immediate repair/replacement of defective seals, in line with national food-safety, energy-performance and workplace-safety rules and manufacturer recommendations. Audit: maintenance logs/checklists showing seal inspections and defrosting in the past year plus repair invoices are presented, and the auditor samples appliance seals and checks for excess ice in a kitchen.',
  '4.19': 'Phasing out ozone-depleting, high-GWP refrigerants reduces emissions and meets international protocols. None of the refrigeration, ventilation, heating and cooling equipment (chillers, heat pumps, split systems, commercial fridges/freezers) purchased within the past 24 months (6 months for first-timers) contains HCFC compounds, including transitional refrigerants such as R-22 and R-123; any second-hand equipment is free of CFC refrigerants, and all equipment complies with national refrigerant phase-out and safety law. Audit: documentation (technical datasheets, purchase records, system specifications) confirming that equipment purchased in the relevant period conforms is presented.',
  '4.20': 'Refrigerants are powerful greenhouse gases, so tracking refills identifies leaks and supports accurate climate accounting. The establishment carries out an annual leak check for all systems containing refrigerants and records every refill in the last full calendar year — at minimum the refrigerant type and the quantity refilled (kg or CO₂e) — signed by a certified technician, with each refill treated as a leakage event in the carbon accounting (criterion 4.27). Small hermetically sealed or low-charge systems are exempt from leak checking but preventive maintenance is recommended, and natural refrigerants are encouraged. Audit: refrigerant logs/reports showing type and amount refilled are presented, or official proof of inspection where no refills occurred.',
  '4.21': 'Heat or cooling recovery captures and reuses energy that would otherwise be wasted. The establishment installs a heat or cooling recovery system — usually a heat exchanger — capturing surplus heat or cooling from major energy-using systems such as ventilation, refrigeration or swimming pools, and reusing it for at least one purpose: preheating incoming ventilation air in cold climates, pre-cooling it in warm climates, heating domestic hot water, or space heating (e.g. indoor parking). Audit: technical specifications or a system operation card showing the recovery system is presented.',
  '4.22': 'Demand-controlled systems provide heating, cooling and ventilation only when and where needed, cutting energy and cost. The ventilation, heating and/or cooling systems are automatically adjusted by sensors according to real-time demand — for example increasing airflow when CO₂ exceeds 1200 ppm or humidity exceeds 70%, or on occupancy detection, and maintaining temperatures within ±3 °C of the setpoint. Time-based on/off scheduling alone does not qualify, as it does not respond to actual conditions. Audit: system logs or equivalent (e.g. building management system screenshots) showing operation under different load conditions over the last 12 months are presented.',
  '4.23': 'Open windows and doors waste energy as HVAC works harder to hold the set temperature. Heating, cooling and ventilation in guest and meeting rooms — and the main dining area for restaurants — automatically shut off when windows and/or doors are opened; the criterion does not apply to permanently sealed windows/doors. Audit: documentation (technical specifications, sensor overviews, area-compliance list) is presented, and the auditor samples a meeting room, guest rooms (and the restaurant dining area) to verify the system switches off when windows/doors are open.',
  '4.24': 'Automatic lighting controls limit electricity use to when spaces are actually occupied. All lighting fixtures in public and staff areas (lobby, corridors, restrooms, staff rooms, kitchens, back-of-house, storage) are equipped with motion detection, timers or automatic dimming/reduction, wherever safe and legal — using passive infrared or ultrasonic sensors, dual-technology sensors, smart controls or scheduled timers, or by dimming when people are not present. Extending smart control to further areas is encouraged. Audit: documentation listing the control type and technology per area is presented, and the auditor samples a public area and a staff area.',
  '4.25': 'Removing in-room mini bars reduces continuous electricity use. The establishment limits mini bars/mini refrigerators in guest rooms, instead offering snacks and drinks from centralised vending, reception or the restaurant/café, so that at least 90% of all guest rooms contain no mini bar or mini fridge. Audit: an overview of guest rooms with room numbers and floors/blocks demonstrating absence in at least 90% of rooms is presented, and the auditor samples guest rooms to confirm.',
  '4.26': 'EV charging supports sustainable mobility and reduces transport emissions. The establishment offers access to charge electric vehicles: charging stations are on the premises (or, where the establishment has fewer than ten parking spots, within 200 m and accessible to guests), and establishments with ten or more parking spots have at least 1 charging station on site. The infrastructure complies with national law, is usable by both guests and staff, and ideally is compatible with multiple vehicle brands. Audit: during the visual inspection the auditor confirms the presence of the EV charging stations.',
  '4.27': 'Calculating greenhouse-gas emissions lets the establishment track its footprint and work toward reductions. The establishment identifies its emission sources and uses its measured energy consumption and other data to estimate its total annual Scope 1 and 2 carbon footprint with a tool aligned to the GHG Protocol, calculating both the last full year and the previous year (last year only for first-timers). At minimum it reports the calculation method and total, Scope 1 and Scope 2 emissions annually, using verified producer-specific emission factors where available. Imperative for hotels, campsites, conference centres and attractions; guideline for small accommodations and restaurants. Audit: the complete calculation with results, input data and method is presented.',
  '4.28': 'Full climate accountability requires assessing indirect emissions across the value chain. The establishment assesses and reports its significant Scope 3 greenhouse-gas emissions in line with the GHG Protocol Corporate Value Chain (Scope 3) Standard — identifying relevant categories (e.g. purchased goods and services, employee commuting, waste, use of sold products) based on a materiality assessment, describing the methodology (spend-based, activity-based or supplier-specific), and using the Protocol’s guidance for factors. Setting reduction targets and engaging suppliers are strongly encouraged. Audit: the complete Scope 3 calculation with results, input data and method is presented.',
  '4.29': 'Setting a carbon-reduction target turns the footprint calculation into proactive climate action. The establishment uses its calculated carbon footprint to set a concrete target to reduce emissions by a defined percentage, specifying the base year, target year, values, scope(s) covered and unit, reviewed and updated annually and prioritising internal Scope 1 and 2 reductions before Scope 3. It identifies the actions needed (energy reduction, efficiency measures, on-site renewables); removals or avoided emissions do not count as gross reductions. Audit: the annual reduction target, methodology and defined actions (which may sit in the action plan) are presented.',

  // ── 5. WASTE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  '5.1': 'Effective waste separation promotes recycling and reuse and supports circular-economy principles. The establishment separates waste per national/local law and, in all cases, into at least 3 recyclable categories (general and hazardous waste do not count), with sorting stations accessible to staff and clearly marked with text and icons, plus at least 1 additional back-office sorting point covering 3 of the same categories. Where infrastructure limits separation, the most impactful streams are prioritised, and reuse is favoured over recycling. Audit: during the visual inspection the auditor confirms correct separation into at least 3 categories, the additional back-office point, clear signage, and correct sorting (bins are opened to check); written confirmation is presented where law dictates combined collection.',
  '5.2': 'Correct downstream handling of separated waste is essential for high-quality recycling. The establishment ensures every separated stream is handled individually after collection — further sorting, recycling or disposal as appropriate — via public authorities, private companies, or its own facilities (e.g. composting or a cardboard compactor). Public/private handling is evidenced by invoices and contracts or written confirmation of separate collection; on-site treatment requires legal compliance, a risk assessment confirming no adverse impacts, and proof of proper processing. Audit: for the relevant period, invoices/contracts (public), invoice plus contract/confirmation (private), or a risk assessment and processing documentation (on-site) are presented, and a bin inspection confirms sufficient collection frequency and capacity.',
  '5.3': 'Guest sorting raises awareness and improves recycling quality. The establishment offers guests a clear, practical way to separate waste — the sorted waste is properly collected and ultimately placed into the establishment’s waste stations, aligned with criterion 5.1. Sorting into at least 3 categories (including general waste) is provided in at least 1 main public area (lobby, conference area, corridors, restaurant), with bins clearly labelled by text and pictograms; in-room sorting is provided in self-catering units. Information for guests is included in the establishment’s general environmental communication. Audit: the auditor confirms a guest-accessible system for at least 3 categories, proper labelling, in-room sorting where applicable, and consistency with the overall waste system.',
  '5.4': 'Hazardous substances and waste pose serious risks to health and the environment if mishandled. The establishment lists the hazardous waste it generates and ensures it is handled per law and Material Safety Data Sheets; stored in sealed, clearly labelled, compatible containers separated by type on drip trays/containment; storage areas inspected regularly for leaks; ideally in a locked, ventilated room; and transported safely to an approved reception facility, preferably by approved public/private carriers. Typical hazardous items include cleaning agents, pool chemicals, pesticides, solvents, cooking oils and fuels, batteries, fluorescent bulbs, toners, e-waste and pharmaceutical waste. Audit: the hazardous-waste list plus evidence of safe transport to an approved facility is presented, and a visual inspection confirms suitable, leak-free containers.',
  '5.5': 'Flushing hygiene items causes blockages and pollution, so accessible bins are needed. A waste bin is available within arm’s reach (no more than 1 metre) of every toilet in guest rooms, staff restrooms and public restrooms, and it is strongly recommended that signage advises users not to flush hygiene or non-flushable items. Audit: the auditor samples a public restroom, a staff restroom and guest rooms to confirm a bin is within arm’s reach of each toilet.',
  '5.6': 'Tracking waste by stream supports reduction, recycling quality and data-driven decisions. The establishment keeps monthly and yearly records of total waste, residual/mixed (non-recyclable) waste and food waste (recording other separated streams is recommended), using a consistent unit by weight or volume, for the last 2 calendar years (last year or 3 months minimum for first-timers). Where monthly data are unavailable, defined estimation methods (quarterly allocation, tracking weeks, or number of collections) are used rather than simply dividing an annual total. Imperative for hotels, campsites, conference centres, restaurants and attractions; guideline for small accommodations. Audit: monthly and annual data for the three streams, showing how the total was calculated, are presented.',
  '5.7': 'Organic waste is a large share of total waste that, well managed, becomes a valuable resource and cuts landfill methane. The establishment separates and reuses organic waste (food scraps, garden waste, coffee grounds, peels, compostable packaging) via at least one route: on-site composting by a trained responsible person with the compost used in landscaping/gardening; collection by an external provider for composting, biogas or other sound processing; or distribution to local farms as animal feed where legally permitted. On-site composting follows good practice (balancing green/brown materials, aeration, moisture, pest protection) and avoids odours. Audit: depending on the route, the composting setup and responsible person, an external-provider contract, or animal-feed documentation is presented, and a source-specific separation system is verified.',
  '5.8': 'Single-use food-service items are resource-intensive and rarely recycled effectively. The establishment does not use single-use food-service items (cups, plates, cutlery, straws, stirrers) for in-house F&B in restaurants, bars, conference areas, room service, guest rooms or tea/coffee stations. Single-use items are only for take-away, standing buffets over 100 guests, or genuine safety-critical settings, and then are certified compostable or non-plastic (paper, cardboard, wood, cornstarch or other plant-based) — never conventional plastic — and not presented as the default. Audit: the auditor confirms no single-use items in guest areas (sampling a storage area) and verifies that any take-away/exception items are non-plastic or certified compostable.',
  '5.9': 'Individually packaged single-serving items generate significant packaging waste. No more than 5 types of F&B products per catering offer are provided in individually packaged single servings across restaurants, cafés, bars, staff canteens and room service (take-away, kiosks and minibars are excluded). Products such as milk, sugar, butter, jam, yoghurt, cheese, salt/pepper, tea and sauces count, though items in the same functional category (all coffees, all jams, all teas, all sugars, all salt/pepper) count as one type; sealed bottled beverages, allergy items, caterer meal trays and ready-to-eat snacks are exempt. Products may not be decanted from single-use servings into reusable containers. Audit: the auditor confirms no more than 5 types of single-serving F&B per catering offer.',
  '5.10': 'Individually packaged toiletries generate plastic and product waste, whereas dispensers cut single-use packaging. Soap, shampoo, conditioner and shower gel are provided in refillable dispensers rather than travel-size containers in guest-room bathrooms, public toilets and staff toilets/showers, and no additional travel-size toiletries of the same type are offered in rooms. Larger single-use or semi-reusable bulk containers count only if the establishment recycles them, its housekeeping SOP requires they be discarded only when completely empty, and supplier refill/reuse is documented where offered; solid soap bars count only if in cardboard/paper with a reuse/recycle arrangement. Audit: the auditor confirms dispensers are present, that no extra same-type toiletries are provided (sampling storage and guest rooms), and reviews recycling/SOP evidence for bulk containers.',
  '5.11': 'Providing single-use amenities only on request reduces unnecessary consumption and packaging waste. Vanity kits and other single-use amenities (shower caps, combs, razors, toothbrushes, cotton buds, slippers, sewing/shaving kits, etc.) are absent from at least 50% of guest rooms and only made available on request at reception or a central service point; a written SOP records which rooms are equipped. No partial-conformity combinations are accepted (e.g. one item on request while others remain in the room). Plastic-free materials, tracking of guest requests, guest information and reusable alternatives are recommended. Audit: the SOP is presented, and the auditor samples guest rooms to confirm at least 50% have no such amenities and that they are available only on request.',
  '5.12': 'Take-away F&B is a major source of single-use packaging that often ends up unrecycled. Unless prohibited by law, the establishment offers, for take-away F&B inside and outside the premises, at least one of: allowing guests to bring and use their own reusable containers; or providing a returnable-container/deposit system (e.g. glass jars, stainless-steel boxes). The option is communicated visibly through posters, menus or digital platforms, and deposit systems or incentives are recommended to encourage participation. Audit: the reusable/returnable-container system is presented, communication materials are reviewed, and availability may be verified on site.',
  '5.13': 'Single-use plastic and disposable materials drive waste, resource depletion and pollution. Unless specific local health regulations make it inapplicable, the establishment does not use or offer disposable bed or bathroom linen (single-use sheets, pillowcases, mattress protectors, duvet covers, towels except hand towels, bathmats); where linen is serviced externally, it is not delivered wrapped in disposable plastic but in reusable trolleys or textile bags. Audit: a storage-area inspection confirms no single-use linen and that externally serviced linen is not plastic-wrapped; supplier confirmation or contracts are presented where delivery cannot be observed, and any health-regulation exemption is verified.',

  // ── 6. PROCUREMENT ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  '6.1': 'Sustainable procurement minimises the harms of tourism operations and influences the wider supply chain. The establishment develops and implements a Sustainable Procurement Policy guiding how environmental, social, cultural and economic sustainability are integrated into all purchasing — capital goods, F&B, durables (furniture, tableware), building materials and services, third-party providers, cleaning products and laundry. It includes commitments to minimise waste and single-use plastics, animal-welfare references where relevant, labour-law expectations (especially in construction), supplier expectations on human rights, and local-labour encouragement; supplier expectations are distributed at least every 2 years and the policy is reviewed every 4 years. Audit: the dated, approved policy and documentation that suppliers received the expectations at least every 2 years are presented.',
  '6.2': 'A consistent approach across the whole site involves third-party businesses on the premises. All third-party operated shops and businesses (hairdressers, cafés, gyms, spas, agencies, souvenir shops, kiosks, bike rentals) are informed at least every 2 years about the establishment’s environmental and social initiatives and Green Key, and encouraged to follow the same principles — waste reduction, energy efficiency, water conservation and sustainable procurement — with training where possible. Where services are owned by the same entity or run under a contract the establishment controls, the lease/agreement includes a clause to follow the applicable Green Key criteria. Audit: information on the third-party businesses plus documentation of the biennial communication (and lease clauses where relevant) is presented.',
  '6.3': 'Eco-labelled and recycled paper reduces deforestation, water use, pollution and emissions. At least 75% of the printing paper, envelopes and printed paper-based materials (menus, key-card holders, leaflets, branded amenity packaging, office and reception paper) purchased or ordered within the past 24 months (6 months for first-timers) carry a recognised eco-label, calculated by quantity of units using purchase records. Audit: documentation (a list or invoices) showing total units ordered and those holding a recognised eco-label to demonstrate the 75% threshold is presented, and the auditor samples 3 items to confirm the eco-labels.',
  '6.4': 'Reducing paper use lowers energy demand, raw-material extraction and waste. The establishment develops an action plan and takes at least 2 initiatives to cut paper use at the front desk, in offices and in guest/meeting rooms — for example limiting or on-request paper in rooms, issuing invoices/receipts only on request, avoiding envelopes, double-sided and eco-default printing, reusing paper, and digital document sharing. An inventory of where paper is used and a voluntary reduction target are recommended, and staff in all relevant departments are involved. Audit: the action plan describing the selected initiatives is presented, and for re-applicants a visual inspection confirms at least 2 measures are in place.',
  '6.5': 'Textiles are resource-intensive, so certified sourcing reduces the footprint and supports fair labour. At least 1 product category of textiles (towels, sheets, pillowcases, duvet covers, tablecloths, napkins, uniforms, curtains) purchased or rented within the past 24 months (6 months for first-timers) carries a recognised product certification addressing environmental or social sustainability — covering raw material (organic, recycled, fair trade), production process, and/or working conditions. Full life-cycle considerations and avoiding harmful chemical finishes apply; second-hand or repurposed textiles are accepted. Imperative for hotels; guideline for campsites, small accommodations, conference centres and restaurants. Audit: purchase/rental records demonstrating a certified textile category are presented, and the auditor samples 3 items to confirm the certifications.',
  '6.6': 'Working closely with suppliers lets the establishment raise environmental and social standards across its supply chain. At least 50% of the establishment’s suppliers hold a recognised third-party eco-certification at the supplier/company level (not merely a certified product) — for example environmental management systems, fair-labour/human-rights, animal-welfare or sustainable-business certifications — across all suppliers it cooperates with during the certification period. Audit: documentation listing all suppliers used and those holding valid eco-certifications, with certificate copies, demonstrating at least 50% is presented, and the auditor samples 3 certificates.',
  '6.7': 'Choosing local or certified laundry reduces transport emissions and the impact of laundry chemicals, water and energy. Where laundry is outsourced, the provider is either located within 100 km of the establishment or holds a valid third-party environmental certification; if no provider exists within 100 km the next closest is used with justification (500 km for very large countries). The establishment remains responsible for minimising the service’s impacts; if laundry is fully in-house the criterion is Not Applicable. Audit: documentation of the provider’s name and address plus contract/invoices, and either proof of location within 100 km, justification for a farther provider, or the provider’s environmental certificate is presented.',
  '6.8': 'Motorised transport contributes significantly to emissions and pollution. At least 75% of the vehicles and transport means owned, rented or leased by the establishment are electric vehicles or cargo-bikes (electric and non-motorised) — covering cars, scooters, golf carts and other vehicles for internal circulation, guest/staff transport and F&B deliveries; contracted taxis, third-party shuttles and public transport are excluded unless exclusively arranged under a recurring contract. Audit: a written overview of all vehicles/transport means with type, energy source and use case is presented, and the auditor samples 3 vehicles.',
  '6.9': 'Staff commuting contributes to local pollution and emissions, so encouraging low-emission travel helps the footprint and staff health. The establishment actively promotes and supports sustainable, health-conscious commuting — through written communication at least every 6 months, engaging posters/materials in staff areas, infrastructure (safe covered bike parking, EV charging), and/or financial or logistical incentives (subsidised transit, free EV charging/parking, shuttles, car-pooling) — highlighting both environmental and health benefits. Audit: evidence of internal communications encouraging sustainable commuting with proof of circulation at least every 6 months, plus documentation of any infrastructure/incentives chosen, is presented.',
  '6.10': 'Digital systems have real environmental impacts across their life cycle (energy, materials and data-related emissions). The establishment takes action to manage IT responsibly — at least 1 measure for under 50 employees, at least 3 for 50 or more — such as optimising digital communication, choosing lower-impact hosting/data services, procuring energy-efficient or refurbished equipment with longer lifespans, encouraging responsible digital use by staff, limiting guest-side digital emissions, running awareness/training on responsible digital practices, and reducing the footprint of the establishment’s website. Audit: a short description or internal policy of the selected measures plus procurement records, equipment inventory or training materials is presented, and the auditor may confirm actions on site.',
  '6.11': 'Extending the life of durable goods reduces demand for new production, waste and emissions. The establishment prioritises prolonging the life of furniture, fixtures, materials and supplies through refurbishment or upcycling (internally or via a third party, extending the item’s life and avoiding a new purchase) and/or donation of items it cannot reuse to charitable or repurposing organisations for further use. Special handling (e.g. data wiping for tech) and basic donation conditions (clean, safe, working) apply; food donations are covered separately. Audit: documentation of refurbishment/upcycling within the past 24 months (6 for first-timers) and/or donation agreements or receipts from the past 6 months is presented.',
  '6.12': 'Certified and local F&B reduces emissions, improves animal welfare and supports local economies and small producers. The establishment purchases and promotes F&B products that are organic, eco-labelled, fair-trade labelled and/or locally produced — at least 5 qualifying products (10 for restaurants) used in significant quantities, covering at least 5 product categories (hot/cold drinks, alcohol, dairy, baked goods, fruit/veg, grains, meat/fish/eggs, condiments). Certifications are recognised by national/international authorities; "local" means SMEs producing within 100 km (500 km for very large countries). Clearly indicating qualifying products is recommended. Audit: a list of the qualifying products across the required categories with certifications or local evidence is presented, and the auditor samples the storage area or restaurant.',
  '6.13': 'Buying fish, seafood or meat from threatened species drives biodiversity loss. As part of its purchasing policy the establishment does not buy fish, seafood or meat from species listed as Vulnerable, Endangered or Critically Endangered on the IUCN Red List, or whose trade is restricted under CITES; recognised seafood guides (e.g. Good Fish Guide, Seafood Watch, WWF) are used and "red/avoid" species avoided, and products from inherently destructive practices (bottom trawling, cyanide fishing) are excluded. Purchasing, menu-planning and kitchen staff know this commitment and use an up-to-date reference guide. Audit: the latest menu plus a list of species used with supplier invoices/records is presented, and a fridge/freezer/storage inspection confirms conformity.',
  '6.14': 'Vegetarian and vegan options reduce the footprint of food services and meet growing guest demand. At least 1 vegetarian starter, 1 vegetarian main and either 1 vegan starter or main are offered across all food services and internally managed outlets, clearly marked on the menu or buffet with icons or other clear means, and made available on request in the staff canteen. Weekly menus offer daily and varied vegetarian/vegan options; fixed menus present vegetarian options; alternative systems (default-vegetarian with meat add-ons) are encouraged. Balanced, minimally processed plant-based dishes are recommended. Audit: the auditor confirms the required vegetarian/vegan dishes are available, clearly indicated, and rotated to avoid repetition.',
  '6.15': 'Reducing food waste cuts the environmental footprint and unnecessary cost, prioritising prevention, then reuse/redistribution, then recycling. The establishment implements at least 2 food-waste actions; most categories also prepare a food-waste reduction plan identifying main sources and concrete measures across preparation and service, ideally with monitoring of quantity, type and cost. Upstream actions include portion control, demand forecasting, menu planning, stock rotation and staff training; downstream actions include reusing leftovers, donating surplus, anti-waste dishes, end-of-service discounts, staff meals and take-away of uneaten food. Guests are informed and involved. Audit: evidence of the implemented actions (training logs, buffet signage, donation records) and, for most categories, the reduction plan is presented.',
  '6.16': 'Offering tap water reduces emissions from bottled water production, transport and packaging. Where local water quality is adequate, tap water is offered at least in restaurants, meeting rooms and staff areas (and may be offered elsewhere); in guest rooms tap water is offered in refilled containers, glasses, or via information on drinkability. Purchasing or serving any single-use externally bottled water does not fulfil the criterion, and bottled water is not the default though it may be available on request. If tap water does not meet national drinking standards the criterion is Not Applicable with documented evidence, and filtration or large reusable dispensers are preferred. Audit: the auditor confirms tap water is available in the required areas; exemption or classification evidence is presented where relevant.',
  '6.17': 'This guideline sets a measured share of sustainable F&B. At least 10% of all F&B products used in the main service areas (breakfast, lunch, dinner, bar/café and internally managed room service) are organic, eco-labelled, fair-trade labelled and/or locally produced, calculated by total weight, volume and/or monetary value over the past 24 months (6 for first-timers). Certifications are recognised by national/international authorities; "local" means SMEs within 100 km (500 km for very large countries). Audit: documentation (a list or invoices) showing total F&B purchased and the qualifying share demonstrating the 10% threshold, plus any local-sourcing justification, is presented, and the auditor samples a storage area or restaurant.',
  '6.18': 'Building on criterion 6.17, this guideline raises the sustainable-F&B share. At least 25% of all F&B products used in the main service areas (breakfast, lunch, dinner, bar/café and internally managed room service) are organic, eco-labelled, fair-trade labelled and/or locally produced, calculated by total weight, volume and/or monetary value over the past 24 months (6 for first-timers), with recognised certifications and "local" meaning SMEs within 100 km (500 km for very large countries). Audit: documentation showing total F&B purchased and the qualifying share demonstrating the 25% threshold, plus any local-sourcing justification, is presented, and the auditor samples a storage area or restaurant.',
  '6.19': 'Reducing meat and increasing plant-based choices lowers emissions and pressure on land and water and supports healthier diets. The establishment either ensures at least 30% of all offered starters and main dishes are vegetarian across internally managed restaurants, buffets and staff canteens, or serves only vegetarian and/or vegan food on at least 1 day per week across all meals and outlets (mandatory for core F&B such as breakfast or half-board even if externally managed). Externally managed restaurants are informed and encouraged in writing to align; the vegetarian offer or day is clearly indicated and communicated positively. Audit: a visual inspection confirms the 30% offer, or the weekly menu shows the vegetarian/vegan day, plus staff-food evidence.',
  '6.20': 'Vegan food has a smaller footprint than meat- and dairy-based food and meets growing demand. The establishment offers at least 1 vegan starter, 1 vegan main dish and 1 vegan dessert across all internally managed restaurants and buffets (recommended in staff canteens), clearly indicated on the menu or buffet with icons or other clear means. Additional vegan options at breakfast are encouraged. Audit: the auditor confirms the establishment offers at least 1 vegan starter, main and dessert, clearly indicated on the menus/buffets.',
  '6.21': 'Frequent linen and towel changes waste water, energy and detergent. There is a sign in the bathroom (towels) and bedroom (bed linen) informing guests that linen and towels are not changed daily by default but follow a reduced-change routine (e.g. every third night) or are changed on request, and that guests can request more or less frequent changes; the information may instead be on the TV, guest binder or a QR code. Informing guests at booking/check-in and preparing beds/towels only for the number of guests booked are recommended. Audit: the auditor checks the signs/information on linen and towel changes in guest rooms.',
  '6.22': 'Daily full-room cleaning consumes large amounts of water, energy and chemicals. The establishment offers guests the option to forego housekeeping (room cleaning, bed-making, towel change) or to select partial cleaning (e.g. bed-making without changing linen), communicated at booking or check-in, via door hangers, QR codes or in-room signage. Incentives (a voucher or contribution to sustainability initiatives) are strongly encouraged, and stricter national hygiene rules are followed where they apply. Audit: evidence of guest communication about the forego-housekeeping/partial-cleaning option is presented; first-time applicants present a draft during the application.',
  '6.23': 'Prioritising effective, environmentally responsible cleaning products lowers the footprint and harmful-substance use. At least 75% of the chemical cleaning products used for routine cleaning (daily, every couple of days, after departure) across the establishment carry a recognised eco-label — covering routine cleaning of guest rooms, conference areas, restaurants, public areas (excluding pools) and staff areas (excluding kitchen and laundry) — calculated by volume or weight from purchase records over the past 24 months (6 for first-timers). Audit: documentation showing the total routine cleaning products and the eco-labelled share demonstrating the 75% threshold is presented, and the auditor samples 3 products.',
  '6.24': 'Overused disinfectants contribute to chemical pollution, antimicrobial resistance and occupational exposure. Routine cleaning of guest rooms, public areas and general surfaces that are not hygiene-critical does not involve disinfection substances; disinfection is restricted to documented cases of clear hygiene or health-critical risk that cannot be handled otherwise, complying with law and used only at the concentration and contact time in the safety data sheets by trained or licensed staff. Hand disinfection is excluded. Audit: the written SOP or integrated cleaning/hygiene procedures identifying which disinfectants are used, where and why are presented.',
  '6.25': 'Tissue paper is widely used and eco-labelling ensures more sustainable sourcing and manufacture. All tissue paper products (paper towels, facial tissues, toilet paper, sauna tissue, napkins, kitchen paper) are certified with a recognised eco-label; where none is available on the market, non-chlorine-bleached paper is accepted with documented proof of unavailability. Conformity is based on purchases over the past 24 months (6 for first-timers). Audit: the auditor samples 3 tissue products to confirm the eco-labels; documented market-unavailability evidence is presented where non-chlorine-bleached paper is used instead.',
  '6.26': 'Dishwashing detergents and rinse agents can pollute water if overused or poorly chosen. The use of dishwashing detergents and rinsing agents is kept to the correct dosage and all such products carry a recognised eco-label, covering both handwashing and machine detergents (soaking, drying and descaling agents excluded), based on purchases over the past 24 months (6 for first-timers). Audit: the auditor samples 3 dishwashing detergents/rinse agents to confirm the eco-labels.',
  '6.27': 'Laundry detergents contribute to water pollution if not used responsibly. The use of laundry detergents is kept to the correct dosage and all such products carry a recognised eco-label (specialty/auxiliary products such as boosters, fabric softeners and stain removers excluded); this applies to in-house laundry, and where laundry is outsourced the establishment encourages the provider to use eco-labelled detergents. Conformity is based on purchases over the past 24 months (6 for first-timers). Audit: the auditor samples 3 laundry detergents to confirm the eco-labels.',
  '6.28': 'Concentrated products with safe dilution reduce packaging waste, transport emissions and chemical overuse. Chemical cleaning products used for routine cleaning are received concentrated and diluted on-site, preferably in closed-loop systems that prevent contact and spills, with an effective and safe dilution system (automatic dosing preferred; controlled manual systems accepted) ensuring accurate dilution; tablet/solid formats are accepted for smaller establishments. Cleaning and laundry staff are informed of correct dosing, with documented training where manual addition is needed. Audit: supplier/product documentation confirming concentrated or tablet products is presented (plus training records for manual dosing), and a visual inspection confirms a functional, calibrated dilution system.',
  '6.29': 'Cosmetic products contribute to chemical pollution, so eco-labelling reduces harmful ingredients. All cosmetic products used by the establishment (soap, shower gel, shampoo, lotion, conditioner) carry a recognised national or international eco-label, in guest-room bathrooms, staff areas and public areas. Products that are also paraben-free and free of Sodium Lauryl Sulphate and unsustainable palm oil are strongly recommended. Audit: the auditor samples 3 cosmetic products to confirm the eco-labels.',
  '6.30': 'Reduced-chemical or chemical-free cleaning lowers the footprint and improves indoor air quality and worker safety. The establishment uses reduced-chemical or chemical-free methods that minimise or eliminate chemical agents for at least 1 defined area, activity or service on a regular basis — for example deionised water, high-pressure water or steam cleaning — for general surface cleaning in areas such as public spaces, guest rooms or conference facilities (kitchens, toilets, pools and spas are exempt due to hygiene rules). Audit: the written SOP for the relevant area/service demonstrating reduced-chemical or chemical-free methods is presented, or a visual inspection confirms the equipment.',
  '6.31': 'Fragrance products add unnecessary chemicals, worsen indoor air quality and risk allergic reactions. The establishment uses only fragrance- and perfume-free products for washing, cleaning and room care — including sprays, detergents and surface cleaners — across all areas (guest rooms, laundry, public areas, conference rooms), whether cleaning is in-house or outsourced. Audit: the auditor samples 3 washing/cleaning/room-care products to confirm no fragrance or perfume is indicated; where cleaning is outsourced, a statement from the third party confirming fragrance-free products is presented.',

  // ── 7. LIVING ENVIRONMENT ──────────────────────────────────────
  '7.1': 'Non-smoking areas protect health and comfort and reduce indoor air pollution. Indoor restaurant areas are non-smoking, and any other public areas are either non-smoking or have a non-smoking section (indoors and outdoors) clearly physically separated from the smoking section — via structural barriers, distance or other measures — so non-smokers are unaffected, with clear signage and fire-safe cigarette disposal in designated smoking areas. A larger non-smoking area than smoking area is recommended. Audit: the auditor confirms smoking is not allowed in indoor restaurants, that other public areas are non-smoking or clearly separated and signed, and that smoking areas have ashtrays/bins with litter-free surroundings.',
  '7.2': 'Smoke-free rooms reduce health risks and keep guest facilities clean. All guest and meeting rooms are non-smoking; where local law or culture makes this infeasible, meeting rooms are non-smoking without exception and at least 75% of guest rooms are non-smoking, with smoking rooms ideally in a physically distinct wing/floor, aired for at least an hour before new guests, clearly signed, and a plan to reach 100% smoke-free. Fire-safe disposal is provided in designated smoking areas. Audit: the auditor confirms all guest and meeting rooms are non-smoking, or (where exceptions apply) that meeting rooms and at least 75% of guest rooms are non-smoking with a plan toward 100% and clear separation and signage.',
  '7.3': 'Regulating staff smoking during working hours protects the health and comfort of staff and guests. Public and staff areas are non-smoking; where staff smoking is allowed, the establishment has a policy setting when and where staff may smoke (covering all cigarettes and pipes), permitting it only in clearly physically separated areas so smoke does not affect others, with fire-safe disposal provided. Progressively strengthening the policy and annual awareness training are recommended, and national smoking legislation is always followed. Audit: the written staff smoking policy is presented, and a visual inspection confirms designated staff smoking areas are physically separated and do not affect non-smokers.',
  '7.4': 'Indoor air quality directly affects the health and well-being of staff and guests. The establishment monitors indoor air quality at least once a year in at least 2 parts of the establishment (choosing areas most at risk, including spas, pools and underground spaces), covering at least 3 parameters from air exchanges, temperature/humidity, CO₂, VOCs, particulate matter, radon and nitrogen oxides. Values stay within national or international limits (e.g. WHO guidelines); where limits are exceeded, responsive low-impact actions are taken, and low-emission options are prioritised when replacing furniture, fixtures and consumables. Audit: documentation showing the monitoring results (and any responsive actions where limits were exceeded) is presented.',
  '7.5': 'Integrating local culture safeguards cultural heritage and supports social sustainability. The establishment incorporates authentic elements of local culture — tangible (materials, construction methods) and intangible (traditional knowledge, cultural expressions) — into its operations or refurbishment/construction, respecting local craftsmanship, artistic expression and design traditions, and involving local expertise. Where cultural expressions are used, appropriate local organisations or experts confirm the use is respectful, and national/local law is followed; durability and long-term sustainability are also considered. Audit: evidence of the relevance of the materials/practices, references to consultation with local experts/community, photos/plans/statements showing incorporation, and a confirmation of respectful use are presented.',
  '7.6': 'Assessing impacts before building minimises harm to ecosystems, soils and water cycles. Before any new development, expansion or major construction/renovation, the establishment demonstrates how environmental and biodiversity impacts were assessed and addressed — via a required ecological/environmental impact assessment, or, where none is required, its own documentation of biodiversity, soil and water-cycle considerations (using experts or public biodiversity maps and tools). Permeable/unsealed surfaces are prioritised over sealed ones; where sealed surfaces are justified, compensatory measures such as rain gardens or infiltration zones are implemented. Audit: documentation of the ecological assessment or equivalent process is presented, and where sealed surfaces exist the justification and compensatory measures are visually confirmed.',
  '7.7': 'Building materials strongly affect energy use, emissions, waste and indoor air quality. For refurbishment or construction in the past 24 months (6 for first-timers), the establishment demonstrates that at least 2 types of materials used meet sustainable requirements: paints/coatings with recognised eco-labels or verified low-VOC and free of heavy metals; wood and plant-based materials with recognised certifications and, where feasible, local sourcing; and/or flooring, insulation, adhesives, sealants or other products with eco-labels, low emissions or recycled content. Prioritising second-hand/recycled materials is recommended where safe and fit-for-purpose. Audit: a list of the sustainable materials used for the 2 selected types plus supporting documentation (invoices, specifications, certificates) is presented, and where appropriate eco-labelled products are visually confirmed.',
  '7.8': 'Restricting agrochemicals reduces risks to ecosystems, biodiversity and human health. Routine use of pesticides (herbicides, insecticides, fungicides, rodenticides, etc.), fertilisers and plant-growth regulators is not permitted; the establishment applies Integrated Pest Management — prioritising preventive and non-chemical methods, then organic/natural alternatives, and only as a last resort agrochemicals (ready-to-use, slow-release, once a year at minimum level), with glyphosate and all FSC-listed Highly Hazardous Pesticides strictly forbidden. Handling is by trained/licensed staff with safety measures, records of application and proper storage; this applies even when green-area maintenance is outsourced. Audit: the written IPM approach is presented (plus application records or the contractor’s policy), and the auditor samples 3 application records against observed practice.',
  '7.9': 'Fuel-powered garden equipment causes emissions, noise and poor air quality. All garden maintenance equipment (lawnmowers, trimmers, leaf blowers and similar) purchased within the past 24 months (6 for first-timers) is energy-efficient, low-noise and low-emission — either manual or electric; robotic mowers should run only in daylight/early evening to avoid disturbing wildlife. This criterion is imperative for establishments with up to 4,000 m² of lawn and guideline for larger areas, and applies even when maintenance is outsourced. Audit: documentation that equipment purchased in the relevant period is electric or manual is presented, plus the contractor’s policy where maintenance is outsourced.',
  '7.10': 'Green areas enhance guest experience and biodiversity but can waste water if poorly managed. The establishment maintains green areas ecologically, adapting practices to local climate and season and supporting biodiversity. Efficient, targeted watering (drip irrigation, moisture sensors, early/late watering) is always mandatory, plus at least 1 additional action from: soil care via composting/mulching; wildlife-timed pruning and mowing (higher settings, undisturbed grass, avoiding nesting seasons); locally adapted, drought-resistant native planting; managing foot/vehicle movement to prevent soil compaction and erosion; and preventing/controlling invasive alien species. This applies to direct and outsourced/landscaping staff. Audit: photos/visual evidence of maintenance practices and installations are presented (plus an SOP or contractor policy where relevant), and the auditor samples areas where watering and additional actions are applied.',
  '7.11': 'Biodiversity underpins ecosystem resilience, local food chains and climate regulation. The establishment implements substantial biodiversity initiatives supporting local (native/indigenous/endemic/rare) species — at least 2 actions if it has green areas (at least 1 in those green areas), or at least 1 action for urban establishments without green areas. Examples include rewilding ≥10% of the outdoor area, pollinator-friendly areas, green roofs/walls, native planting, nesting boxes/insect hotels/beehives/wildlife corridors, protecting local habitats, eliminating ecological traps, supporting biodiversity associations, urban-greening collaborations, and hosting biodiversity events. This applies to direct and outsourced staff. Audit: the biodiversity SOP and a record of interventions (with biennial review for re-applicants) are presented, plus any contractor policy, and the auditor samples a green area where on-site initiatives are taken.',
  '7.12': 'Understanding on-site biodiversity and nature-related risks is a prerequisite for responsible land management. The establishment undertakes a biodiversity and nature-related risk-and-opportunity assessment establishing a baseline and highlighting sensitive areas — identifying species and habitats, ecological corridors, wetlands and functional soils; pressures from human activity (landscaping, light/noise, invasive species); dependencies on ecosystem services and risks; and opportunities for improvement, ideally with site mapping. It covers only areas under the establishment’s control; sites with green areas larger than 5 ha use an external expert, while smaller sites may assess internally using public resources. Audit: the written assessment covering the required elements, with the references/tools used, is presented.',
  '7.13': 'Regular observation strengthens ecological learning, engages staff and guests, and contributes to biodiversity data. The establishment carries out regular observation and documentation of biodiversity-supporting features and species on or around its premises at least once a year — for example using a simple biodiversity analysis grid, participating in citizen-science protocols, uploading observations, submitting data to national databases, running nature walks/monitoring days, or inviting an ecologist or conservation NGO. Conformity is based on consistent effort to observe, document and reflect, not on specific outcomes; species catalogues and identification guides are recommended. Audit: evidence of the monitoring activities carried out (observation notes, completed grids, species lists, photos or app/citizen-science screenshots) is presented.',
  '7.14': 'Food systems drive climate change, biodiversity loss and resource use, so green-area food initiatives raise awareness and shift behaviour. The establishment engages staff, guests and/or the community through at least 1 awareness-raising initiative in its green areas linked to food production, food waste, biodiversity or local sourcing — for example a permanent fruit/herb/vegetable garden, greenhouse or raised beds, an outdoor cooking/garden-to-table area, a wild meadow or food forest, an educational native-plant garden, or on-site beehives managed with a local expert. The initiative is clearly part of operations, serves an educational function and is integrated into the guest experience, following the pesticide/fertiliser (7.8) and maintenance (7.10) criteria; revenue-generating agriculture is out of scope. Audit: records of the initiative, species grown, and how it engages guests/staff/community are presented, and a visual inspection confirms its presence and integration.',
}
